Consider the following statements&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><strong>a.</strong></td><td>In crystallization, an impure compound is dissolved in a solvent in which it is highly soluble at room temperature.&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>b.</strong></td><td>In the positive&nbsp;resonance effect(+R), the transfer of electrons is always from an atom or substituted group attached to the conjugated system.&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td><strong>c.</strong></td><td>One of the technological applications of fractional distillation is to separate different fractions of crude oil in the petroleum industry.</td></tr></tbody></table>
The correct statements are:&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1" style="min-width: 300px;"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td><strong>(a)</strong> and <strong>(b)</strong> only&nbsp;</td><td>2.</td><td><strong>(b)</strong> and <strong>(c)</strong> only&nbsp;</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(a)</strong> and <strong>(c)</strong> only&nbsp;</td><td>4.</td><td><strong>(a)</strong>, <strong>(b)</strong>, and <strong>(c)</strong>&nbsp;</td></tr></tbody></table>

Given below are two statements:&nbsp;
<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Assertion&nbsp;(A):</strong></td><td>Sulphur present in an organic compound can be estimated quantitatively by the Carius method.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Reason&nbsp;(R):</strong></td><td>Sulphur is separated easily from other atoms in the molecule and gets precipitated as a light yellow solid.</td></tr></tbody></table>
&nbsp;

<table border="1" cellpadding="1" cellspacing="1"><tbody><tr><td>1.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>2.</td><td>Both&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;are True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is not the correct explanation of&nbsp;<strong>(A)</strong>.</td></tr><tr><td>3.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is True but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is False.</td></tr><tr><td>4.</td><td><strong>(A)</strong>&nbsp;is False but&nbsp;<strong>(R)</strong>&nbsp;is True.</td></tr></tbody></table>
